    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Can You Jump Off the Boat on a Boat Trip in Madeira?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>4 December 2026</span><span>6 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Yes, on the Day Trip you can jump in at the swim stop, once the skipper has stopped the boat, turned the engine off and pointed to the spot. The Sunset Trip has no swimming, so there is no jumping on it either.</p>

      <h2>When are you allowed to jump in?</h2><p>Only at a swim stop on the Day Trip, and only after the skipper says go. Never in the marina, never while the boat is moving, and never on the Sunset Trip.</p><p>The <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip west along the south coast</a> has two planned stops in the water. The first is Fajã dos Padres, a ledge of orchards and vines squeezed under a huge cliff, where you can swim, jump in and take out the paddle boards. The second is off Ribeira Brava, for another swim before the fast run home. On the 3 hour version the boat carries on for 30 minutes more to Ponta do Sol.</p><p>The Sunset Trip is built around the evening light and a glass on deck. Nobody goes in the water on it. If jumping is the reason you want a boat, pick the Day Trip and tell Chifbay when you book.</p><h2>How deep is the water where you jump?</h2><p>Much deeper than any jump from a boat deck needs. Madeira rises steeply out of deep ocean, so the sea floor falls away fast off this coast, and a skipper stops for a swim in water where nobody can stand.</p><p>Depth is rarely the danger when you jump from a boat. What sits beside your landing spot matters more: the hull, a paddle board drifting under you, a swimmer who surfaced in the wrong place, a rope in the water. That is why the skipper picks the side of the boat you jump from, not only the bay.</p><p>Tides do not change much out here. The range in Funchal is about 1 to 2 metres, which matters on rocks and in natural pools, not under a boat sitting in deep water. Jumping from rocks ashore is a different game with different risks, and our <a href="/posts/cliff-jumping-in-madeira-guide">guide to cliff jumping in Madeira</a> covers it.</p><h2>Where on the boat do you jump from?</h2><p>From the side or the bow, wherever the skipper points. Keep away from the back: the ladder and swim platform are there, and that is the way people climb back in.</p><p>The drop from a deck to the water is short, a step and a push rather than a leap. That makes it a good first jump for nervous swimmers and a quick, fun one for everyone else. The movements that keep it safe are the same every time.</p><ol><li><strong>Wait for the engine to stop.</strong> The skipper tells you when the boat is settled and the water is clear.</li><li><strong>Look down before every jump.</strong> The patch of water where you will land must be empty: no swimmer, no board, no line.</li><li><strong>Push away from the hull.</strong> Step off with a little push so you land clear of the boat's side.</li><li><strong>Go feet first.</strong> Arms close to the body, or one hand holding your nose. Lifesaving groups give the same advice for any new stretch of water: the first time in, go in feet first.</li><li><strong>Swim clear, then signal.</strong> Come up, move a few strokes away from the landing spot and give a wave so the next person knows you are fine.</li></ol><p>One person at a time. A second jumper landing on the first is the most common way a fun stop turns into a bruise. Diving head first is a different matter: ask the skipper before you try it, and accept the answer.</p><h2>How do you get back on board?</h2><p>By the ladder at the stern, one at a time, when the skipper says so. Swim to it slowly, take hold with both hands and climb with your weight close to the ladder.</p><p>Two small things help. Keep your hands and feet away from the underside of the swim platform, which moves with the swell. And save some energy: after five or six jumps and climbs, arms get tired, and the last climb is the one people find hardest. If you feel cold or tired, that is the moment to stop, not one more jump later.</p><p>Back on deck, walk, do not run. A wet white deck and bare feet are a slippery mix, and the boat moves even at anchor.</p><h2>When does the skipper say no?</h2><p>Whenever the sea, the spot or the person makes it a bad idea. The call can come before anyone is in the water or halfway through the stop, and it is final.</p><ul><li><strong>Swell.</strong> A swell from the west or south west can make the boat lift and drop, and the ladder becomes hard to use. Then the skipper may move to a calmer corner, shorten the stop or skip the jumping.</li><li><strong>Wind.</strong> A breeze off the land can push swimmers and paddle boards away from the boat faster than people expect.</li><li><strong>Traffic.</strong> Other boats arriving, fishing lines or swimmers too close to where you would land.</li><li><strong>Something in the water.</strong> Floating jellyfish such as the Portuguese man o' war appear on this coast in some seasons, and a skipper will not send people in among them.</li><li><strong>Drink.</strong> Britain's lifeboat charity, the RNLI, puts it plainly: nobody should jump into water after drinking. Swim first, toast afterwards.</li></ul><p>On a day when the sea is wrong for the whole coast, the trip simply does not run, and your group moves to a different day or gets the whole amount back. Calling it off is the skipper's job, and a skipper who does it is doing that job well.</p><h2>Who should think twice before jumping?</h2><p>It depends on the person, and saying no is a perfectly normal choice. Some people come for the jumping. Others prefer to slide in from the ladder and float.</p><p>Skip the jump, or ask the skipper first, if you have back or neck trouble, recent ear problems or surgery, or you are pregnant. Children can jump only with an adult watching, at the spot and the moment the skipper agrees, and only if they swim well. Tell us their level when you book, and a Chifbay skipper will plan the stop around them.</p><p>If someone in your group cannot swim, they can still enjoy the stop from the platform. Read <a href="/posts/boat-trip-in-madeira-if-you-cannot-swim">what non-swimmers can do on the boat</a> before you book. If you need a buoyancy aid of a particular size, ask on WhatsApp before booking rather than assuming.</p><h2>Is the water cold when you land?</h2><p>Cool, not cold. Long-term averages put the sea around Funchal at about 18°C from January to April, rising to about 23 or 24°C in August and September.</p><p>That is warmer than the 15°C the RNLI treats as cold water, but the first breath after a jump in March still catches. If you are unsure, climb down the ladder the first time to feel the temperature, then jump. In late summer most people simply go.</p><p>For more on staying safe once you are in, read <a href="/posts/how-to-swim-safely-from-a-boat-in-madeira">how to swim safely from a boat in Madeira</a>. For the main stop itself, the <a href="/faja-dos-padres-boat-trip">Fajã dos Padres boat trip</a> page shows where Chifbay anchors for the swim.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Can children jump off the boat in Madeira?</h3><p>Yes, if they swim well, with an adult watching, and only where and when the skipper agrees. Tell us their swimming level before the trip.</p><h3>Can you jump in during the evening trip?</h3><p>No. Nobody goes in the water on the Sunset Trip. Book the Day Trip for the swims, the jumps and the paddle boards.</p><h3>How do you climb back onto the boat after jumping?</h3><p>By the ladder at the stern, one person at a time, once the skipper gives the go.</p><h3>Can you dive head first from the boat?</h3><p>Ask the skipper first. Feet first is the safe way in for anyone who does not know the spot.</p>

      <blockquote>Look down, push away from the hull, feet first, then swim clear for the next one.</blockquote>
    </article>
    <p class="pref-src" data-pref-source><span>Enjoying our Madeira guides?</span> <a href="https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=chifbay.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="#4285F4" d="M23.5 12.3c0-.8-.1-1.6-.2-2.3H12v4.5h6.5a5.6 5.6 0 0 1-2.4 3.6v3h3.9c2.2-2.1 3.5-5.1 3.5-8.8z"/><path fill="#34A853" d="M12 24c3.2 0 6-1.1 7.9-2.9l-3.9-3c-1.1.7-2.4 1.2-4 1.2-3.1 0-5.7-2.1-6.6-4.9h-4v3.1A12 12 0 0 0 12 24z"/><path fill="#FBBC05" d="M5.4 14.4a7.2 7.2 0 0 1 0-4.7V6.6h-4a12 12 0 0 0 0 10.8l4-3z"/><path fill="#EA4335" d="M12 4.8c1.7 0 3.3.6 4.6 1.8l3.4-3.4A12 12 0 0 0 1.4 6.6l4 3.1C6.3 6.9 8.9 4.8 12 4.8z"/></svg>Add Chifbay as a preferred source on Google</a></p>

